    type: "qa",
    q: "When would a seller choose a broad auction, a targeted auction or a negotiated sale?",
    a: "It depends on how well the seller knows who will pay most. A broad auction reaches dozens of strategic and financial buyers when the likely winner is unclear. A targeted auction approaches a handful of logical buyers, keeping tension with less exposure. A negotiated sale fits when one buyer clearly values the company most or speed and secrecy matter more.",
    why: "Every extra bidder adds competition and cost. A broad process gives the best chance of finding an unexpected buyer, but it's slow and eats management time with weak bidders. It also leaks more easily and spreads sensitive data to more parties, competitors included. A targeted process cuts those costs when the seller can name the buyers most likely to pay the most. A negotiated sale is fastest and most private but gives up competitive tension, so a public company's board typically relies on a fairness opinion and on deal terms that still let a rival bid after signing, sometimes a go-shop.",
    trap: "The follow-up: why accept a pre-emptive bid mid-auction? When it's clearly above what the process is likely to produce and comes with certainty, such as committed financing and a quick signing. Buyers pre-empt to avoid a bidding war.",
