    id: "ib-process-005",
    track: "ib",
    module: "ib-process",
    topic: "The CIM",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What's in a CIM, and how much can a buyer rely on it?",
    a: "The confidential information memorandum is the seller's detailed selling document, often 50 pages or more: investment highlights, the business, its market, customers, operations, management, historical financials and management's projections. Buyers get it after signing an NDA. Legally it offers little protection: it disclaims accuracy, and the purchase agreement usually says the buyer relies only on the reps inside it.",
    why: "The CIM is marketing built on facts. The bank drafts it with management to tell the company's story at its best, and the projections are usually management's optimistic case. It lets buyers form a first-round valuation without the data room, so sensitive details stay back until the field narrows. Before it comes a one- or two-page teaser, often anonymous, that tests interest. The NDA limits how buyers use what they see, usually bars them from poaching employees and, for a public target, often adds a standstill that stops a bidder buying shares or going hostile.",
    trap: "The follow-up: how would you treat the CIM's projections? As the seller's upside case. Build your own case, test the plan against the company's track record and trim growth or margins that the history doesn't support."
